Apple's fine print on the <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/109503">Logic Pro release notes page</a>:<blockquote>The intelligent assistant (beta) requires an Apple Creator Studio subscription, a Mac with Apple silicon, and macOS 27 or later.</blockquote>Indeed, the release notes for the one-time purchase version of Logic Pro for Mac version 13.0 do not mention Intelligent Assistant.<br/>
<br/>
The subscription requirement was not mentioned in <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/apple-creator-studio-adds-powerful-new-ai-features-and-even-more-creative-tools/">Apple's announcement</a> last month:<blockquote>The assistant (beta) in Logic Pro for Mac requires macOS 27 Golden Gate or later on an Apple Intelligence-capable Mac and is available in U.S. English only.</blockquote>Apple customers who are growing tired of features being locked behind a subscription will obviously be disappointed to learn this news.<br/>
<br/>
Logic Pro's Intelligent Assistant likely requires a Creator Studio subscription because it uses cloud AI models. In a <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/127888">support document</a>, Apple said the feature will have monthly usage limits, with expanded usage available for a fee.<br/>
<br/>
